# Deliberately empty.
#
# Bun auto-loads `.env` for `bun test`, so a developer's real credentials —
# COMPOSIO_API_KEY and friends — land in the test process. Tests written to
# assert the *unconfigured* path then see a configured one and fail, but only
# on machines that run the app; CI has no `.env`, so it stays green and the
# failure looks like "my branch is broken".
#
# Worse than the flakiness: a test that reads a live broker key can make real
# API calls from a local run.
#
# ── The rule ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `--env-file` binds to ONE bun process. It replaces that process's dotenv
# auto-load; it does not propagate. So **every bun process in the test path
# passes it** — the runner, and anything the runner spawns:
#
#   bun test --env-file=test/.env.test <files>     # the test:* scripts
#   bun run  --env-file=test/.env.test <file>      # spawned children
#
# A child re-runs the auto-load on its own, so a `bun run` spawned from a
# cleaned parent gets the key straight back (see `test/integration/cli.test.ts`,
# which boots the full runtime). Running a single file by hand is the same case:
# pass the flag yourself.
#
# What the rule does not reach, because it is about dotenv and not the
# environment: a value exported in your shell (direnv and friends) already
# outranks `.env` and survives regardless. `test:web` and `test:bundles` run in
# their own directories and do not pass the flag — neither reads a credential
# today, but they are the same shape if that changes.
#
# There is no repo-wide switch. `bunfig.toml` has no env-file key, and a
# `[test]` preload that scrubbed the environment would also hit `bun run eval`,
# which legitimately needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from `.env`.
